@Andrzej With respect they are not the same grammatically (not sure what you mean by etymologically.) 'False' is an adjective. A false idol is an idol that is false. 'Teen' is a noun, used as a modifier. A teen idol is an idol TO teens. The idol is not teen, because that's meaningless.

The generalized 'this is how crossword clues work', or the fact that this takes the shape of many crossword clues, does not excuse it not making sense. The clue is 'it may be false or teen'; an IDOL may in fact be false, but an IDOL may not be teen, which is precisely what the clue says it may. Conversely, to use one of Matt's examples, 'it may be false or golden' would be a totally logical clue (and quite good actually!); 'false' and 'golden' are both adjectives; an idol may in fact be false, or it may in fact be golden. 'Matinee' is again a noun modifier that wouldn't work in this construction; a matinee idol is an idol SEEN IN matinees; a idol cannot BE 'matinee.'

As I mentioned, another extremely common clue formula, 'word with false or teen', is one that would make total sense here. That implies merely, as Matt suggests, that the words go together in a phrase, which they do. But 'it may be' implies a more exact logical relationship that does not exist here. Do you see the difference?

